<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">St. Mary's City, Maryland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1634">1634</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St. Mary's City is the site of the first English settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, founded in March 1634 by colonists who arrived on the ships Ark and Dove under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-calvert">Leonard Calvert</a>, near the mouth of the Potomac. It was the colony's capital until 1694, when the government moved to Annapolis, after which the town declined. Its archaeological remains now form an outdoor history museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-marys-city-maryland.jpg" alt="St. Mary's City, Maryland" width="320" height="470" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acroterion · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Mary%27s_State_House,_Maryland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The town stood in the south of St. Mary's County, at the southern tip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>'s western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chesapeake-bay">Chesapeake Bay</a>, beside the St. Mary's River, a short tidal tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potomac-river">Potomac River</a>. The Piscataway people were the area's original inhabitants and helped the first colonists. The colony was the plan of George Calvert, who had returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> in 1625 and hoped to found a haven for persecuted Catholics; it was the first settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> founded with a mandate to give refuge to both Catholics and Protestants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tobacco, the colony's main export, brought a boom and drove the spread of African chattel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; older ways for enslaved people to gain freedom were abolished. The town built a state house, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> chapel, a jail and an inn. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> a Protestant raiding party attacked the town in 1644, burning buildings and plundering Catholics who would not renounce their faith, a period colonists called the Plundering Time; afterwards only about 100 people remained. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-calvert">Leonard Calvert</a> raised a militia and retook the town in 1647.</p><p>In 1649 the assembly at St. Mary's passed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-toleration-act">Maryland Toleration Act</a>, the first law to mandate tolerance among Christian sects, which stayed in force for 40 years. A brick statehouse was finished in 1676. Falling tobacco prices from the 1660s sharpened tension between Protestant planters and the Catholic leadership, and in 1689 Protestant settlers rose against the Lords Baltimore; the Crown took over the colony, Catholics lost the vote in 1692, and the new governor, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-nicholson">Francis Nicholson</a>, moved the capital to Annapolis.</p><p>The remaining farms were later merged into the Brome-Howard slave plantation, which ran through most of the 19th century. During the Civil War Union troops burned Brome's Wharf to stop trade with the Confederacy across the Potomac. In 1840 St. Mary's Seminary, a nondenominational school for young women, was founded on the site as a living monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> and to help heal the Protestant-Catholic tensions that still divided the county.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town is now Historic St. Mary's City, a state-owned outdoor museum with reconstructed buildings, a working colonial farm and a replica of the Dove. Excavation began in 1971, and the National Park Service has called it probably the most intact 17th-century English town in the country represented entirely by archaeology. Finds include the William Nuthead printing house, the first in the Southern colonies, a 1645 fort, the foundation of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> chapel, three 17th-century lead coffins, and St. Mary's Fort of 1634, unearthed in late 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St. Mary's City, Maryland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1634 the ships Ark and Dove brought the first settlers, led by Leonard Calvert, to <b>St Mary's City</b> on the lower Potomac.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-maryland">Province of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
